The next objective is to find any files that are possible writable to 
find / -writable -type f 2>/dev/null | grep -vE '^/(proc|sys|run)' | head -40
Two files stand out 
/opt/admin_bot.py — writable, owned by root
/opt/monitoring/health_report.sh — writable

Before I triaged both paths I found that /opt/tools/pspy64 was available to run. By discoving this I was able to see the active processes and when they ran. 
After looking at the data health_report.sh would fire at the top of every minute making this an excellent target for malicious code 
UID=0  /bin/sh -c /opt/monitoring/health_report.sh

Two facts combined to make this exploitable:

UID=0 — root’s cron is executing the script, so every command inside the script runs with root’s privileges.
-rwxrwxrwx — the script is world-writable, so you (devops, an unprivileged user) can change what’s inside it.

Now here is the payload 
echo 'cp /bin/bash /tmp/rootbash && chmod 4755 /tmp/rootbash' >> /opt/monitoring/health_report.sh
Why this works: 

cp /bin/bash /tmp/rootbash — copies the bash binary to a new file you own the location of. Here’s the key: 
when root runs this, root creates the copy, so the copy is owned by root. A file’s owner is whoever creates it, and cron is running as root. 
I now have a root-owned copy of bash sitting in /tmp.
chmod 4755 /tmp/rootbash — this is the heart of it. That 4 in front of 755 is the SUID bit (Set User ID).
755 = owner can read/write/execute, group and others can read/execute (standard).
The leading 4 sets SUID.

Once the health file was executed I checked to see if the rootbash file was created
ls -la /tmp/rootbash
-rwsr-xr-x 1 root root 1446024 Nov  5 21:18 /tmp/rootbash
This result shows that the file was created succefully and the payload was effective 
